def rawMdx : DocSlug -> String
  | .introduction => "---\ntitle: \"Introduction to Lakeport\"\nsummary: \"What Lakeport is, why it pairs a pure Lean 4 core with native platform primitives, and when to choose it.\"\nsection: \"Getting started\"\norder: 1\nreadingMinutes: 5\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Introduction to Lakeport\n\nLakeport is a cross-platform application framework written in Lean 4. You write your state, reducers, routing, wire codecs, views (`.lp` TSX), and stylesheets (`.css`) once. From that single project, Lakeport compiles your application logic to WebAssembly for browsers and to C for native desktop and mobile shells.\n\nIf you have built apps with React Native, Elm, or Flutter, the mental model will feel familiar right away. Your interface is a pure function of your state:\n\n```lean title=\"The Lakeport loop\"\nState -> StyledView Event Field\n```\n\nWhen someone clicks a button or types into a text field, the host shell sends an event into your Lean reducer. The reducer computes the next state and a list of outbound effects (an HTTP call, a clipboard write, a WebSocket frame). Lakeport diffs the view tree and patches only the native or DOM nodes that changed.\n\n## Why Lean 4 for application code?\n\nMost UI frameworks stop at type-checking props and JSON shapes. That catches typos, which helps in day-to-day work, yet it leaves the hardest bugs untouched: conservation of balances, offline retry ordering, stale server capabilities during a rolling deploy, and state transitions that violate an invariant five clicks deep.\n\nLean 4 is both a fast functional programming language (compiling to C with Perceus reference counting) and an interactive theorem prover. In Lakeport, those two halves live in the same file tree:\n\n- **Your app is ordinary functional code.** You define structures, inductive sums, and `.lp` TSX components much like you would in TypeScript or Elm. You do not need to write a single proof to get a working app on screen.\n- **When a rule matters, you state it as a theorem.** Because your reducer is a pure function with no hidden mutation or ambient `IO`, Lean's kernel can verify properties about the exact `step` function your users execute in production.\n- **Proofs cost zero bytes at runtime.** Every `Prop` and every `theorem` is erased before C and WebAssembly code generation. Modules that contain only theorems (`*Laws.lean`) compile to zero bytes of object code.\n\n<Callout kind=\"important\" title=\"What proofs cover (and what they do not)\">\nA Lean theorem proves a property of your pure model, wire codec, or reducer under explicit hypotheses. It does not prove that a browser engine, operating system kernel, or remote server is bug-free. Good Lakeport code keeps the imperative host shell small and pushes every real decision across the boundary into pure Lean.\n</Callout>\n\n## Why coding agents thrive in Lakeport\n\nIf you pair-program with coding agents, you have probably noticed failure modes that unit tests miss. An agent fixes a flaky retry test by clearing a pending flag early, or adds a wildcard `_ => pure ()` branch to silence a compiler warning. All twenty test cases pass, and then a customer gets billed twice on a spotty train Wi-Fi connection.\n\nLakeport turns business constraints into compile-time walls that an agent cannot talk its way past:\n\n1. **Exhaustive versioned contracts.** Edge handlers match every wire version without wildcard arms. An unimplemented case fails `lakeport check`.\n2. **Kernel-checked invariants.** If `escrow_conserves_cents` says `available + held + released = 10000` across every action, a buggy retry patch will fail to compile until the invariant holds for *all* inputs, not merely the three inputs in a test file.\n3. **Banned escape hatches.** Release checks inspect the compiled Lean environment for `sorryAx` and custom axioms. An agent cannot stub out a hard proof obligation and declare victory.\n\n## Platform maturity at a glance\n\nLakeport renders through real platform primitives rather than a custom Skia canvas: DOM nodes on the web, GTK4 on Linux, CoreGraphics/AppKit on macOS, UIKit on iOS, Direct2D on Windows, and Android Views on Android.\n\n| Target | Host shell | Current status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Web (SPA + SSG) | Compiled `wasm32` + DOM renderer | Production baseline, static pre-rendering and in-place hydration |\n| Linux desktop | C core + GTK4 | Desktop preview (`lakeport build linux`) |\n| macOS / iOS | C core + AppKit / UIKit | Native shell preview (`lakeport build macos`, `lakeport build ios`) |\n| Windows / Android | C core + Direct2D / Android Views | Native shell preview (`lakeport build windows`, `lakeport build android`) |\n\n## How to read this manual\n\nIf you are new to Lakeport, read the next four pages in order:\n\n- [Installation](/docs/installation) sets up the Lean 4 toolchain and `lakeport` CLI.\n- [Your first app](/docs/getting-started) builds a working counter and task list from scratch in ten minutes.\n- [Versioned union contracts](/docs/versioned-contracts) shows how to type the boundary between your client and server so old clients and new servers coexist peacefully.\n- [Frontend-first releases](/docs/frontend-first-releases) explains our deployment rule of thumb: ship the client with its capability gate closed, then roll out the backend.\n"
  | .installation => "---\ntitle: \"Installation and setup\"\nsummary: \"Install elan, verify the pinned Lean 4 toolchain and WebAssembly target, and run lakeport doctor.\"\nsection: \"Getting started\"\norder: 2\nreadingMinutes: 4\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Installation and setup\n\nLakeport ships as a single native CLI binary (`lakeport`) alongside three core Lean packages (`lakeport-style`, `lakeport-router`, and `lakeport-effects`). This page walks through installing the prerequisites on Linux and macOS and verifying that WebAssembly builds work on your machine.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\nBefore installing Lakeport, make sure you have:\n\n- **Git** and **curl** for fetching toolchains and Reservoir packages.\n- **elan**, the Lean version manager, which automatically downloads the exact Lean 4 compiler pinned in a project's `lean-toolchain` file.\n- **Clang 16+** with the `wasm32` target enabled (standard in Homebrew LLVM on macOS and `clang` + `lld` packages on Ubuntu/Debian) plus `wasm-opt` from Binaryen for release builds.\n- **Node.js 20+** if you use static site generation (`mode = \"ssg\"`), because `lakeport build web` runs your compiled `.wasm` binary once at build time to pre-render every route into static HTML.\n\n## Step 1: Install elan\n\nIf you do not already have `elan` installed, run the standard installer script and add `~/.elan/bin` to your `PATH`:\n\n```sh title=\"Terminal\"\ncurl https://elan.lean-lang.org/elan-init.sh -sSf | sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.elan/bin:$PATH\"\n```\n\nVerify that `lean` and `lake` respond:\n\n```sh title=\"Terminal\"\nlean --version\nlake --version\n```\n\n## Step 2: Check your environment with lakeport doctor\n\nClone or extract the Lakeport archive, put `packages/lakeport-cli/bin` on your `PATH`, and run `lakeport doctor`:\n\n```sh title=\"Terminal\"\nlakeport doctor\n```\n\n`lakeport doctor` inspects your local compilers, linkers, Binaryen optimizer, and platform SDKs, then prints a plain checklist showing which targets are ready to build right now. You only need the tools for the targets you actually compile: if Android Studio is not installed, `lakeport build web` and `lakeport build linux` still work normally.\n\n<Callout kind=\"tip\" title=\"Keep build scratch directories off /dev/shm\">\nLean and Clang write temporary object files during compilation. On Linux machines with small `/dev/shm` tmpfs mounts, set `TMPDIR` to a directory on your main disk (for example `export TMPDIR=\"$HOME/.cache/lakeport/tmp\"`) so parallel builds never run out of memory-backed disk space.\n</Callout>\n\n## Step 3: Editor support\n\nLakeport uses `.lean` files for pure logic and proofs, `.lp` files for typed TSX views, and `.css` files for stylesheets.\n\n1. Install the official **Lean 4** extension in VS Code, Cursor, or Neovim (`lean4-mode` / `lean.nvim`) for live tactic state views on `.lean` files.\n2. Run `lakeport check` in watch mode or wire `lakeport mcp` into your editor agent so diagnostics on `.lp` and `.css` files map straight back to the exact line and column in your source file.\n\n## Project layout\n\nA newly initialized Lakeport repository is deliberately flat:\n\n```sh title=\"Directory layout\"\nmy-app/\n  lakefile.toml       # Project manifest, routes, and target settings\n  assets/\n    icon.svg          # Vector app icon used across web and native shells\n  shared/             # Optional leaf package for shared client/server contracts\n  src/\n    app.css           # Global design tokens and layout classes\n    Routes.lean       # Typed URL codec and LawfulUrlCodec proof\n    State.lean        # Pure state, actions, and step reducer\n    StateLaws.lean    # Proof-only module ([proof-only, 0 B C])\n    App.lp            # TSX views and StyledApp entry point\n```\n\nNext, head to [Your first app](/docs/getting-started) to wire these pieces together and run your first build.\n"
  | .gettingStarted => "---\ntitle: \"Your first app\"\nsummary: \"Build a Release Checklist app from an empty folder, add a state invariant, and pre-render static HTML.\"\nsection: \"Getting started\"\norder: 3\nreadingMinutes: 8\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Your first app\n\nThe fastest way to learn Lakeport is to build a small working app and break it once on purpose. Over the next few minutes we will create a Release Checklist app with three pieces:\n\n1. A pure state machine in `src/Checklist.lean`.\n2. A one-screen TSX interface in `src/App.lp` styled by `src/app.css`.\n3. A short theorem in `src/ChecklistLaws.lean` proving that checking items off never changes the total item count.\n\n## 1. Create the project manifest\n\nCreate a new directory called `release-checklist` and write `lakefile.toml` at its root:\n\n```toml title=\"lakefile.toml\"\nname = \"release-checklist\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndefaultTargets = [\"ReleaseChecklist\"]\n\n[[lean_lib]]\nname = \"ReleaseChecklist\"\nsrcDir = \"src\"\nroots = [\"App\"]\n\n[lakeport]\nbundleId = \"dev.example.checklist\"\napplicationId = \"dev.example.checklist\"\nentry = \"App\"\napp = \"App.app\"\ncss = \"src/app.css\"\noutDir = \"dist\"\nnodeCapacity = 16384\n\n[lakeport.web]\ntitle = \"Release Checklist\"\nmode = \"ssg\"\nthemeColor = \"#0f172a\"\nssgRoutes = [\"/\"]\n```\n\nNotice `entry = \"App\"` and `app = \"App.app\"`. That tells Lakeport to compile `src/App.lp` and mount the `StyledApp` value named `App.app`.\n\n## 2. Model the state and reducer\n\nCreate `src/Checklist.lean`. We keep all state transitions here in ordinary Lean so they are easy to read, test, and prove things about without touching any UI code.\n\n```lean title=\"src/Checklist.lean\"\nnamespace Checklist\n\nstructure Item where\n  id : Nat\n  label : String\n  done : Bool := false\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited\n\nstructure Model where\n  items : List Item := [\n    { id := 1, label := \"Ship frontend capability gate\", done := true },\n    { id := 2, label := \"Deploy dual-version server\", done := false },\n    { id := 3, label := \"Enable v2 capability flag\", done := false }\n  ]\n  commit : Nat := 0\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited\n\ninductive Action where\n  | toggle (id : Nat)\n  | resetAll\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr\n\ndef toggleItem (target : Nat) (it : Item) : Item :=\n  if it.id == target then { it with done := !it.done } else it\n\ndef step (m : Model) : Action -> Model\n  | .toggle id =>\n    { items := m.items.map (toggleItem id), commit := m.commit + 1 }\n  | .resetAll =>\n    { items := m.items.map (fun it => { it with done := false }), commit := m.commit + 1 }\n\ndef completedCount (m : Model) : Nat :=\n  (m.items.filter (fun it => it.done)).length\n\nend Checklist\n```\n\nThere are no hooks, observables, or mutable refs here. `step` takes a `Model` and an `Action` and returns the new `Model`.\n\n## 3. Write the view in .lp TSX\n\nNow create `src/App.lp`. A `.lp` file is a Lean module where JSX tags lower directly to `LakeportStyle.StyledView` calls. Every class name in `class=\"...\"` is checked against `src/app.css` during `lakeport check`; if you misspell a class name, compilation fails with the exact line and column.\n\n```lp title=\"src/App.lp\"\nimport Checklist\nset_option synthInstance.maxSize 4096\n\nnamespace App\nopen LakeportStyle Checklist\n\nabbrev Event := Nat × Nat\nabbrev V := StyledView Event Nat\n\n@[default_instance] instance {E F : Type} : ToChildren (List (StyledView E F)) E F :=\n  ⟨fun xs => xs.zipIdx.map fun (v, i) => (some i, v)⟩\n\ndef rowView (it : Item) : V :=\n  <div class=\"row\" data-done={it.done}>\n    <button class=\"hit\" onClick={(1, it.id)}>{it.label}</button>\n    <span class=\"box\">{if it.done then \"x\" else \"\"}</span>\n    <span class=\"label\">{it.label}</span>\n  </div>\n\ndef view (m : Model) : V :=\n  <main class=\"shell\">\n    <header class=\"head\">\n      <h1 class=\"title\">Release checklist</h1>\n      <p class=\"sub\">{toString (completedCount m) ++ \" of \" ++ toString m.items.length ++ \" complete\"}</p>\n    </header>\n    <section class=\"list\">\n      {m.items.map rowView}\n    </section>\n    <button class=\"reset\" onClick={(2, 0)}>Reset checklist</button>\n  </main>\n\ndef press (m : Model) (ev : Event) : Model × List Lakeport.Signals.Term :=\n  match ev with\n  | (1, id) => (step m (.toggle id), [])\n  | (2, _) => (step m .resetAll, [])\n  | _ => (m, [])\n\ndef app : StyledApp Model Unit Event Nat where\n  initial := fun _ => {}\n  view := view\n  encodeEvent := fun (tag, id) => .pair (.nat tag) (.nat id)\n  decodeEvent := fun | .pair (.nat tag) (.nat id) => some (tag, id) | _ => none\n  encodeField := .nat\n  decodeField := fun | .nat n => some n | _ => none\n  press := press\n  edit := fun m _ _ => some (m, [])\n  message := fun m _ => (m, [])\n  decodeNamed := fun _ _ => .error (.codec .badTag)\n  commandImage := fun _ => none\n  appCommit := fun m => m.commit\n  descriptor := \"release-checklist/1\"\n  profile := ⟨⟨32, 16384⟩, ⟨32, 16384⟩⟩\n\nend App\n```\n\n<Callout kind=\"note\" title=\"Why buttons contain text labels\">\nIn Lakeport, `<button>` carries an accessible text label only rather than arbitrary nested `<div>` trees. That rule guarantees every button has a real accessible name on iOS VoiceOver, Android TalkBack, GTK, and the DOM alike. To make a rich card clickable, place `<button class=\"hit\" onClick={...}>{label}</button>` inside a `position: relative` container alongside visual `<span>` children styled with `pointer-events: none`.\n</Callout>\n\n## 4. Add a stylesheet\n\nCreate `src/app.css` defining `.shell`, `.head`, `.title`, `.sub`, `.list`, `.row`, `.hit`, `.box`, `.label`, and `.reset`:\n\n```css title=\"src/app.css\"\n* { box-sizing: border-box; }\nbody { margin: 0; background: #f8fafc; color: #0f172a; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; }\n.shell { max-width: 520px; margin: 48px auto; padding: 24px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; }\n.head { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px; }\n.title { font-size: 24px; font-weight: 700; }\n.sub { font-size: 14px; color: #475569; }\n.list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; }\n.row { position: relative; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px; padding: 12px 14px; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 8px; }\n.row[data-done=\"true\"] { background: #f1f5f9; }\n.hit { position: absolute; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0; left: 0; opacity: 0; cursor: pointer; }\n.box { width: 20px; height: 20px; border: 1px solid #64748b; border-radius: 4px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; pointer-events: none; }\n.label { font-size: 15px; pointer-events: none; }\n.reset { align-self: flex-start; padding: 8px 14px; border-radius: 6px; background: #0f172a; color: #ffffff; }\n```\n\n## 5. Prove your first invariant\n\nCreate `src/ChecklistLaws.lean`. Because `App.lp` does not import `ChecklistLaws` and `ChecklistLaws.lean` contains only `theorem` declarations, Lakeport checks the proof during every build and drops the module to `0` bytes of C and WebAssembly.\n\n```lean title=\"src/ChecklistLaws.lean\"\nimport Checklist\n\nnamespace Checklist.Laws\nopen Checklist\n\ntheorem step_preserves_item_count (m : Model) (a : Action) :\n    (step m a).items.length = m.items.length := by\n  cases a <;> simp [step]\n\nend Checklist.Laws\n```\n\n## 6. Check and build\n\nRun `lakeport check` for fast feedback, then build the release WebAssembly and pre-rendered HTML bundle:\n\n```sh title=\"Terminal\"\nlakeport check .\nlakeport build web . --release\n```\n\nInspect `dist/web/index.html`. Lakeport executed your compiled `app.wasm` at build time, wrote the initial `<main class=\"shell\">...</main>` markup directly into `index.html`, and attached `boot.mjs` so the browser hydrates the existing DOM nodes in place on commit `0` without tearing down or recreating a single element.\n\nNow that you have the loop under your fingers, read [Versioned union contracts](/docs/versioned-contracts) to see how Lakeport structures real production apps that talk to a server.\n"
  | .versionedContracts => "---\ntitle: \"Versioned union contracts at the edge\"\nsummary: \"Put an explicit Version sum and dependent Request/Response family in a shared leaf package so old clients and new servers never drift.\"\nsection: \"Recommended patterns\"\norder: 4\nreadingMinutes: 9\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Versioned union contracts at the edge\n\nIn a single-server web app from 2008, you deployed one tarball and everyone got the new code on their next page load. Modern client apps do not get that luxury. A browser tab can stay open for four days, a Service Worker or CDN edge can serve yesterday's bundle during a deploy, and a mobile app binary can sit un-updated in someone's pocket for six months.\n\nAt the boundary of your application, **two versions of your program are almost always running at the same time**.\n\nMost teams treat that boundary informally: optional JSON fields, ad-hoc `\"v2\"` URL prefixes, and defensive `if (resp.receipt !== undefined)` checks scattered across thirty components. Over a year, nobody knows which fields are actually guaranteed to exist, and coding agents quietly invent half-compatible payloads that crash whichever side was deployed earlier.\n\nLakeport's first core pattern is straightforward: **model every wire version explicitly as an inductive union inside a shared leaf package, index your requests and responses by that version, and forbid wildcard arms in handlers.**\n\n## 1. Create a shared leaf package\n\nPut your edge contract in `shared/` with zero UI imports (`Init` and `LakeportEffects` only). Both your client app and your backend (or contract conformance harness) depend on `shared/`:\n\n```toml title=\"shared/lakefile.toml\"\nname = \"site-contract\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndefaultTargets = [\"SiteContract\"]\n\n[[lean_lib]]\nname = \"SiteContract\"\nsrcDir = \".\"\nroots = [\"SiteContract\"]\n\n[lakeport]\nkind = \"sdk\"\n```\n\nBecause `shared/` cannot import `LakeportStyle` or `App.lp`, view concerns never leak into wire types.\n\n## 2. Separate wire versions from capabilities\n\nA protocol version (`v1`, `v2`) names a wire framing and baseline request/response shape. A capability (`instant_release`, `audit_receipt`) names an optional feature that a server may enable or withdraw independently. Keep both in `Versions.lean`:\n\n```lean title=\"shared/SiteContract/Versions.lean\"\nnamespace Site.Contract\n\ninductive Version where\n  | v1\n  | v2\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited\n\ninductive Capability where\n  | instantRelease\n  | auditReceipt\n  | batchSettle\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited\n\nstructure Advertisement where\n  versions : List Version\n  capabilities : List Capability\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited\n\nend Site.Contract\n```\n\nWhen the client boots, it fetches `GET /api/capabilities` and parses the server's `Advertisement` **fail-closed**: unknown schema numbers, empty version lists, or unrecognized capability tokens return `none` rather than guessing.\n\n## 3. Index Request and Response by Version\n\nInstead of one giant struct full of `Option` fields that \"only exist in v2\", give each version its own honest `ClientFrame` and `ServerFrame` inductive type, then tie them together with a dependent type family:\n\n```lean title=\"shared/SiteContract/Api.lean\"\ndef Request : Version -> Type\n  | .v1 => Wire.V1.ClientFrame\n  | .v2 => Wire.V2.ClientFrame\n\ndef Response : Version -> Type\n  | .v1 => Wire.V1.ServerFrame\n  | .v2 => Wire.V2.ServerFrame\n\nstructure Handler (v : Version) where\n  encode : Request v -> String\n  decode : String -> Option (Response v)\n\ndef clientHandler : (v : Version) -> Handler v\n  | .v1 => { encode := Wire.V1.encodeReq, decode := Wire.V1.decodeResp }\n  | .v2 => { encode := Wire.V2.encodeReq, decode := Wire.V2.decodeResp }\n```\n\nLook closely at `clientHandler`. Notice two things:\n\n1. Its return type `Handler v` **depends on the value `v`**. It is a type error to wire `Wire.V1.encodeReq` into the `.v2` branch because `Request .v2` is `Wire.V2.ClientFrame`, not `Wire.V1.ClientFrame`.\n2. There is **no `| _ =>` wildcard branch**. If an engineer or coding agent adds `| v3` to `Version` tomorrow, `lakeport check` immediately flags `clientHandler`, `negotiateVersion`, and every server handler with a missing-cases error.\n\n## Interactive contract simulator\n\nTry switching server advertisements and wire commands below. Everything in this widget runs inside the actual `Site.Contract` Lean code compiled into this page's WebAssembly binary:\n\n<ContractSimulator />\n\n## 4. Prove codec round-trips once\n\nHand-rolled serializers break when a separator character appears inside user text. Use `LakeportEffects.WirePack` (`packFields` / `unpackFields`) for canonical escaping and add a short `*Laws.lean` file proving `decode (encode msg) = some msg` for every version:\n\n```lean title=\"shared/SiteContract/ContractLaws.lean\"\ntheorem v2_decodeReq_encodeReq (f : V2.ClientFrame) :\n    V2.decodeReq (V2.encodeReq f) = some f := by\n  cases f <;>\n    simp [V2.encodeReq, V2.decodeReq, WirePack.unpackPair_packPair, parseDecimalNat?_repr]\n```\n\n## 5. Keep the three version axes independent\n\nA mistake we learned the hard way is coupling wire versions to local storage schemas or bundle versions. Keep all three axes separate:\n\n| Axis | Where it lives | What bumps it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Wire protocol (`Version.v1`, `Version.v2`) | `shared/SiteContract/` | Changing request/response framing over HTTP or WebSocket |\n| Local persistence (`schema_v1`, `schema_v2`) | Client outbox / KV store | Changing how offline drafts or cached rows are stored on disk |\n| App release (`1.4.0`, `1.4.1`) | `lakefile.toml` | Shipping UI fixes, CSS updates, or new client logic |\n\nYou should be able to ship ten client releases on `Version.v2` without touching the wire contract, and migrate local KV storage without asking the backend team to coordinate a deploy.\n\n## 6. Retiring an old version cleanly\n\nNever delete `Version.v1` on a hunch. First remove `.v1` from the backend's `Advertisement` while leaving the `v1` code compiled in. Watch production telemetry until `v1` request volume hits zero across your longest client update window. Only then delete `| v1` from `Version` and let Lean's compiler guide you to every dead branch you can safely remove.\n\nNext, read [Frontend-first releases](/docs/frontend-first-releases) to see how versioned contracts pair with staged rollouts so deploys never race each other.\n"
  | .frontendFirstReleases => "---\ntitle: \"Always release the frontend first\"\nsummary: \"Why shipping a capability-gated client before enabling new backend behavior eliminates rollout races and makes rollbacks boring.\"\nsection: \"Recommended patterns\"\norder: 5\nreadingMinutes: 8\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Always release the frontend first\n\nWhen a feature spans both client and server, teams usually ask: *\"Do we deploy the backend first or the frontend first?\"*\n\nIn Lakeport, our default answer is **always release the frontend first**, with new UI paths and outbound commands gated behind server-advertised capabilities that start **off**.\n\nAt first glance that sounds backwards. Why ship frontend code for a backend feature that is not turned on yet? Walk through what happens during an ordinary rolling deploy and the reason becomes clear.\n\n## Why \"backend first\" still bites you in rolling deploys\n\nSuppose you are adding **Instant Escrow Release** (`Version.v2` + capability `instant_release`). If you deploy the new backend and turn the behavior on immediately while clients are only halfway rolled out, or if you deploy an ungated frontend that assumes every server speaks `v2`:\n\n1. **Load-balancer split brain.** During a 10-minute rolling deploy, half of your backend pods run the old binary and half run the new one. An ungated client hits a new pod on page load, then hits an old pod on its next `POST` and gets a `400` or `404`.\n2. **Offline outbox replay.** A mobile user queues an `instantRelease` action while on `v2`, walks into an elevator, and reconnects after you rolled the backend back to fix an unrelated memory leak. Without a capability gate on replay, the outbox hammers the rolled-back server with frames it cannot parse.\n3. **Painful rollbacks.** If the backend migration misbehaves and clients are already assuming the new contract unconditionally, rolling back the server breaks active client sessions.\n\n## The four-step frontend-first rollout\n\nInstead, separate **shipping code** from **turning behavior on**:\n\n1. **Step 1: Ship the frontend first (gate closed).** The new client understands both `Version.v1` and `Version.v2`, knows how to render the Instant Release button, and knows how to encode `V2.ClientFrame.instantRelease`. Because the current production server only advertises `versions = [v1], capabilities = []`, the client negotiates `v1`, hides the Instant Release button, and blocks `instantRelease` at the command gate. User-visible change: zero.\n2. **Step 2: Deploy the dual-version server (capability still off).** Roll out the backend binary supporting both `v1` and `v2`, advertising `versions = [v1, v2], capabilities = []`. Updated clients shift cleanly to `v2` baseline framing while older cached clients keep speaking `v1` without error.\n3. **Step 3: Enable the capability advertisement.** Once all backend instances are healthy, flip server config to advertise `capabilities = [instant_release]`. On their next capability refresh, clients reveal the Instant Release UI and `permitsV2Command` opens.\n4. **Step 4: Roll back by withdrawing the capability (if needed).** If metrics spike, remove `instant_release` from `GET /api/capabilities`. Clients hide the control and hold or downgrade unnegotiated commands without requiring an emergency frontend redeploy or mobile App Store review.\n\n## Try the rollout scrubber\n\nStep through all four stages below to watch what the server advertises, what an older cached client does, and what the newly released Lakeport client permits at each stage:\n\n<RolloutTimeline />\n\n## Guard the command boundary as well as the JSX\n\nHiding a button in your `.lp` view is good ergonomics, yet it is **not** a safety boundary. A keyboard shortcut, a stale timer callback, or an offline outbox flush can still fire an action when the button is not on screen.\n\nAlways check `permitsV2Command` (or `permitsEncodedFrame`) inside your pure reducer before emitting an outbound `EffectReq`:\n\n```lean title=\"Guarding both direct clicks and outbox retries\"\ndef sendV2 (ad : Option Advertisement) (nextId : Nat) (connId : Nat)\n    (cmd : Wire.V2.ClientFrame) : Option EffectReq :=\n  if permitsV2Command ad cmd then\n    some (.wsSend nextId connId (Wire.V2.encodeReq cmd))\n  else\n    none\n```\n\nNow you can state a two-line theorem in your `*Laws.lean` file that holds regardless of what your UI components do:\n\n```lean title=\"Kernel-checked rollout gate\"\ntheorem unnegotiated_instant_never_emits (ad : Option Advertisement)\n    (hNoCap : permitsV2Command ad (.instantRelease cid cents) = false) :\n    sendV2 ad nextId connId (.instantRelease cid cents) = none := by\n  simp [sendV2, hNoCap]\n```\n\n<Callout kind=\"warning\" title=\"When backend-first is required\">\nThere is one classic exception: **additive server-side tolerances** before a client stops sending a legacy field, or adding a database index before a client queries it. Even then, the rule is the same underlying principle: *deploy tolerances and gates first, activate new traffic last.*\n</Callout>\n\nNext, read [Pure core and Lean proofs](/docs/pure-core-and-proofs) to see how to structure your application reducer so invariants stay short, readable, and fast to prove.\n"
  | .pureCoreAndProofs => "---\ntitle: \"Pure core, explicit effects, and Lean proofs\"\nsummary: \"Structure your reducer so Lean theorems verify real production code with 0 sorry, 0 custom axioms, and zero runtime overhead.\"\nsection: \"Recommended patterns\"\norder: 6\nreadingMinutes: 10\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Pure core, explicit effects, and Lean proofs\n\nTeams hearing about formal verification for the first time usually worry about two failure modes:\n\n1. *\"Will every CSS tweak require a three-hour Coq proof?\"*\n2. *\"What stops someone from proving a toy helper function while the real app runs completely different code?\"*\n\nBoth worries are valid. Lakeport's architecture is designed specifically to avoid both traps.\n\n## Prove invariants of your domain, not pixel coordinates\n\nYou never write theorems about margin widths or hover colors. Instead, split your `src/` folder along a clean seam:\n\n- **Domain modules (`src/*.lean`)** hold your `State`, `Action`, `WellFormed : State -> Prop`, and pure `step : State -> Action -> State × List EffectReq`.\n- **Law modules (`src/*Laws.lean`)** import your domain modules and prove `theorem` declarations about `step`.\n- **View modules (`src/*.lp`)** import your domain modules and render `State` to `StyledView`.\n\nBecause `App.lp` calls the exact `step` function that `StateLaws.lean` proves theorems about, **there is no gap between the specification model and the executed reducer**. At the same time, you can redesign `App.lp` and `app.css` ten times a day without touching a single proof.\n\n## A concrete conservation invariant\n\nHere is a simplified escrow reducer where money sits in one of three buckets: `available`, `held`, or `released`. A bug that duplicates or loses cents during a retry is catastrophic.\n\n```lean title=\"src/Escrow.lean\"\nstructure Ledger where\n  available : Nat := 10000\n  held : Nat := 0\n  released : Nat := 0\n  inFlight : Bool := false\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr\n\ndef TotalCents (l : Ledger) : Nat :=\n  l.available + l.held + l.released\n\ninductive Action where\n  | reserve (cents : Nat)\n  | settleHeld\n  | socketDropped\n  deriving DecidableEq\n\ndef step (l : Ledger) : Action -> Ledger\n  | .reserve cents =>\n    if !l.inFlight && cents <= l.available then\n      { l with available := l.available - cents, held := l.held + cents, inFlight := true }\n    else l\n  | .settleHeld =>\n    { l with held := 0, released := l.released + l.held, inFlight := false }\n  | .socketDropped =>\n    { l with inFlight := true }\n```\n\nNotice `.socketDropped`: when a WebSocket closes mid-flight before the server acknowledges whether the reserve succeeded, we **keep `inFlight := true`** (uncertain delivery) until reconciliation completes rather than blindly clearing the lock and allowing a duplicate debit.\n\nNow look at the conservation theorem in `EscrowLaws.lean`:\n\n```lean title=\"src/EscrowLaws.lean\"\nimport Escrow\n\nnamespace Escrow.Laws\nopen Escrow\n\ntheorem step_conserves_cents (l : Ledger) (a : Action) :\n    TotalCents (step l a) = TotalCents l := by\n  cases a with\n  | reserve cents =>\n    unfold step\n    split\n    · rename_i hOk\n      simp only [Bool.and_eq_true, decide_eq_true_eq] at hOk\n      simp [TotalCents]\n      omega\n    · rfl\n  | settleHeld =>\n    simp [step, TotalCents]\n    omega\n  | socketDropped =>\n    rfl\n\nend Escrow.Laws\n```\n\nLean's built-in `omega` tactic discharges the natural-number subtraction (`cents <= l.available` implies `(available - cents) + (held + cents) + released = available + held + released`) automatically. Notice what happens if you remove `cents <= l.available` from `step`: `Nat` subtraction truncates at zero, `omega` refuses the goal, and Lean points right at the underflow bug.\n\n## Always write one productive witness theorem\n\nSafety theorems (\"nothing bad happens\") have a classic blind spot: a reducer that ignores every click and returns `l` unchanged trivially conserves cents!\n\nPair every safety invariant with at least one **productive witness theorem** showing that valid user sequences actually reach the intended state:\n\n```lean title=\"Productive witness in EscrowLaws.lean\"\ntheorem reserve_then_settle_releases :\n    let l0 : Ledger := {}\n    let l1 := step l0 (.reserve 2500)\n    let l2 := step l1 .settleHeld\n    l2.available = 7500 ∧ l2.held = 0 ∧ l2.released = 2500 := by\n  decide\n```\n\nIf an overzealous capability gate accidentally rejects every command, `reserve_then_settle_releases` breaks immediately.\n\n## Rules for [proof-only, 0 B C] modules\n\nLakeport classifies a `.lean` module as `[proof-only, 0 B C]` and omits its C translation unit from native and WebAssembly linking when two conditions hold:\n\n1. No runtime module reachable from `App.lp` imports it.\n2. The file declares `theorem` or `lemma` items and **zero** computational definitions (`def`, `abbrev`, `structure`, `inductive`, `class`, `instance`, `opaque`, `axiom`, or `@[extern]`).\n\n<Callout kind=\"tip\" title=\"Where to put Prop predicates\">\nPut `Prop` definitions like `def WellFormed (s : State) : Prop := ...` inside your domain file (`State.lean`), not inside `StateLaws.lean`. Because `WellFormed` returns `Prop`, Lean erases it to zero bytes inside `State.lean`, and `StateLaws.lean` stays strictly `theorem`-only so Lakeport drops the entire law module from the C/Wasm link!\n</Callout>\n\n## What Lakeport audits on release builds\n\nWhen you run `lakeport check` or `lakeport build web --release`, keep three rules non-negotiable:\n\n- **`0 sorry` (`sorryAx`):** Unfinished proofs never ship.\n- **`0` custom `axiom` declarations:** No invented axioms hiding unproved assumptions.\n- **`0 Lean.ofReduceBool` (`native_decide`):** Use kernel-checked `decide`, `rfl`, `simp`, `cases`, and `omega` so the Lean kernel itself verifies every step without trusting native code evaluation inside the compiler.\n\n## Naming the trust boundary honestly\n\nWe avoid marketing badges like \"100% Verified\" or artificial `L0-L4` assurance grades because a real system always has both proved pure code and an unproved runtime boundary. Document both sides plainly in your repo:\n\n- **Kernel-checked:** URL round-trips (`LawfulUrlCodec`), wire frame round-trips, capability gates, conservation/well-formedness across `step`, and `MemoCell` view cache coherence.\n- **Trusted computing base (tested, not kernel-proved):** Lean 4's C code generator, `clang --target=wasm32`, `boot.mjs` / `dom-renderer.mjs` (or the native GTK/AppKit/UIKit/Android shell), and the host OS/browser.\n\nNext, move on to [Views and .lp TSX](/docs/views-and-tsx) to learn the practical details of building fast, accessible interfaces in Lakeport.\n"
  | .viewsAndTsx => "---\ntitle: \"Views, .lp TSX, and memoization\"\nsummary: \"Write typed JSX in Lean, understand accessible hit overlays and keyed lists, and cache expensive subtrees with MemoCell.\"\nsection: \"Building interfaces\"\norder: 7\nreadingMinutes: 8\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Views, .lp TSX, and memoization\n\nLakeport `.lp` files let you write familiar JSX markup directly inside Lean 4 modules. Before the Lean compiler runs, `lakeport` lowers every JSX expression into typed `LakeportStyle.StyledView` constructors and writes a line-and-column source map so compiler errors point straight at your `.lp` file.\n\n## Supported HTML and vector primitives\n\nBecause a single `.lp` view can render to Web DOM, GTK4, CoreGraphics, Direct2D, UIKit, and Android Views, Lakeport focuses on a compact, well-specified set of semantic elements:\n\n- **Layout and landmarks:** `div`, `span`, `header`, `section`, `main`, `aside`, `footer`, `nav`\n- **Typography:** `h1`, `h2`, `h3`, `p`\n- **Controls:** `button`, `input`, `textarea`\n- **Media and graphics:** `img` (with required `src` and `alt`) and `svg` (containing `path`, `circle`, `rect`, `line`, `polyline`, `polygon`, `ellipse`, `g`, `defs`, `linearGradient`, and `clipPath`)\n\n## Accessible buttons and hit overlays\n\nIn HTML it is common (and tempting) to stuff three `<div>`s and an unnamed `<svg>` inside a `<button>` or attach `onClick` to a `<div>`. Screen readers on mobile and desktop struggle with both habits.\n\nLakeport enforces two rules at compile time:\n\n1. `onClick={event}` is valid **only on `<button>`**.\n2. A `<button>` renders a plain text label (`String`) so every interactive target has an unambiguous accessible name across every platform.\n\nWhen you want a rich card, navigation tab, or icon button to be clickable, layer an invisible `<button class=\"hit-overlay\">` inside a `position: relative` container and give sibling visual elements `pointer-events: none`:\n\n```lp title=\"Accessible icon + badge button pattern\"\ndef navTab (active : Bool) (label : String) (count : Nat) (ev : Event) : V :=\n  <div class=\"nav-tab\" data-active={active}>\n    <button class=\"hit-overlay\" onClick={ev}>{label}</button>\n    <span class=\"nav-label\">{label}</span>\n    <span class=\"nav-count\">{toString count}</span>\n  </div>\n```\n\nKeyboard focus, Enter/Space activation, and VoiceOver/TalkBack labels all attach to the real `<button>`, while your visual layout stays completely flexible.\n\n## Controlled text inputs\n\n`<input>` and `<textarea>` bind to a typed field identifier (`F` in `StyledView E F`) via the `field={...}` attribute:\n\n```lp title=\"Controlled search field\"\n<input\n  class=\"search-input\"\n  field={1}\n  value={state.query}\n  placeholder=\"Filter documentation...\"\n  aria-label=\"Filter documentation\"\n/>\n```\n\nWhenever the user edits the input, the host shell invokes `app.edit state fieldId newValue`, returning the updated `State` and any outbound effects.\n\n## Keyed lists\n\nWhen rendering a `List` or `Array` of child views, Lakeport needs a stable `Nat` key per child so the reconciler updates moved or edited rows in place. You can attach `key={item.id}` explicitly on each element, or provide a module-local `ToChildren` instance for static positional lists:\n\n```lean title=\"Positional keys for static lists\"\n@[default_instance] instance {E F : Type} : ToChildren (List (StyledView E F)) E F :=\n  ⟨fun xs => xs.zipIdx.map fun (v, i) => (some i, v)⟩\n```\n\nUse explicit `key={item.id}` whenever rows can be reordered, inserted at the head, or deleted mid-list.\n\n## Proved subtree memoization with MemoCell\n\nIn JavaScript frameworks, `useMemo` trusts you to list every dependency in an array; forget one and your UI displays stale data.\n\nBecause Lean functions are pure, `LakeportStyle.MemoCell α β f` stores `(input : α)`, `(output : β)`, and an erased proof `coherent : output = f input`. On a cache hit (`c.input = x`), `c.refresh x` returns `c` untouched with zero heap allocations:\n\n```lean title=\"Caching parsed MDX views in application state\"\ndef renderDocSlug (slug : DocSlug) : MdxDoc :=\n  LakeportMdx.parseMdx (Docs.rawSource slug)\n\nstructure DocsCache where\n  current : MemoCell DocSlug MdxDoc renderDocSlug :=\n    MemoCell.init renderDocSlug .introduction\n```\n\nEven better, `MemoCell.refresh_get` is a kernel-checked theorem (`(c.refresh x).get = f x`), so caching can never cause your view to diverge from recomputing from scratch, and the `coherent` field erases to zero bytes at runtime.\n\nNext, read [Styling and .lpcss](/docs/styling-and-lpcss) to see how Lakeport compiles CSS with zero runtime style recalculation overhead.\n"
  | .stylingAndLpcss => "---\ntitle: \"Styling, CSS modules, and .lpcss\"\nsummary: \"Compile-time verified CSS classes, scoped import_css modules, data-attribute state styling, and native .lpcss bundles.\"\nsection: \"Building interfaces\"\norder: 8\nreadingMinutes: 6\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Styling, CSS modules, and .lpcss\n\nIn Lakeport, stylesheets are ordinary `.css` files checked at compile time rather than untyped strings concatenated at runtime. One stylesheet drives both the browser (`bundle.css`) and native desktop/mobile targets (`bundle.lpcss`).\n\n## Compile-time class verification\n\nWhen you write `class=\"card card-header\"` in a `.lp` file, Lakeport checks every token against your global stylesheet (`[lakeport] css = \"src/app.css\"`) and built-in utility tokens before Lean even runs.\n\nIf you rename `.card-header` to `.panel-header` in `src/app.css` and miss a reference in `src/Marketing.lp`, `lakeport check` stops immediately:\n\n```sh title=\"Compile-time CSS diagnostic\"\nsrc/Marketing.lp:42:10: error[css-global]: unknown CSS class \"card-header\":\n  not defined by the global stylesheet and not a supported utility\n```\n\nDead typos never survive to production.\n\n## Scoped CSS modules for reusable packages\n\nGlobal stylesheets work well inside an application, yet reusable Reservoir packages (such as `lakeport-mdx`) should never pollute a consumer's global class namespace.\n\nAny `.lp` file can import a co-located stylesheet with `import_css`:\n\n```lp title=\"packages/lakeport-mdx/src/LakeportMdx/Render.lp\"\nimport LakeportMdx.Ast\nimport_css MdxCss from \"./Mdx.css\"\n\ndef badge (lang : String) : StyledView E F :=\n  <span class={MdxCss.langBadge}>{lang}</span>\n```\n\nLakeport hashes and scopes every selector in `Mdx.css` per package and generates typed `ClassName` bindings (`MdxCss.langBadge`, `MdxCss.codeCard`, and so on). Misspelling `MdxCss.langBage` fails compilation with a clear hint naming `./Mdx.css`.\n\n## Dynamic state with data-* attributes and CSS variables\n\nNotice that `class=\"...\"` literals are static. Instead of building brittle string templates like `` `btn ${isActive ? 'btn-active' : ''} ${theme}` ``, keep `class=\"btn\"` static and pass dynamic state through typed `data-*` attributes and `--*` CSS custom properties:\n\n```lp title=\"Static class + typed data attributes\"\n<div class=\"gate-pill\" data-open={gateOpen} --pill-accent={accentHex}>\n  <span>{if gateOpen then \"Permitted\" else \"Blocked by Gate\"}</span>\n</div>\n```\n\nThen style those states cleanly in CSS:\n\n```css title=\"src/app.css\"\n.gate-pill {\n  padding: 4px 10px;\n  border-radius: 9999px;\n  background: rgba(220, 38, 38, 0.12);\n  color: #dc2626;\n}\n.gate-pill[data-open=\"true\"] {\n  background: rgba(13, 148, 136, 0.14);\n  color: var(--pill-accent, #0d9488);\n}\n```\n\n## Light and dark themes without duplication\n\nDefine all color and surface tokens as CSS custom properties on your root `.site-shell` class, then override **only the tokens** under `.site-shell[data-theme=\"dark\"]` (or `[data-theme=\"light\"]`). Every component reads `var(--bg-surface)`, `var(--ink-primary)`, and `var(--border-subtle)`, so both themes stay synchronized without duplicating layout rules.\n\nNext, read [Routing and static site generation](/docs/routing-and-ssg) to wire typed URLs and zero-create hydration.\n"
  | .routingAndSsg => "---\ntitle: \"Routing, LawfulUrlCodec, and SSG\"\nsummary: \"Parse and format URLs with a proved inverse codec, manage back-stacks purely, and pre-render static HTML from compiled Wasm.\"\nsection: \"Building interfaces\"\norder: 9\nreadingMinutes: 8\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Routing, LawfulUrlCodec, and SSG\n\nBroken internal links and mismatched client/server routes are completely avoidable when URLs are backed by an inductive type and a round-trip proof.\n\n## 1. Define your Route sum and UrlCodec\n\nStart in `src/Routes.lean` by listing every valid screen in your application as an inductive type:\n\n```lean title=\"src/Routes.lean\"\ninductive SiteRoute where\n  | home\n  | whyLean\n  | ecosystem\n  | showcase\n  | docsIndex\n  | docsPage (slug : DocSlug)\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr, Inhabited\n```\n\nNext, implement `LakeportRouter.UrlCodec SiteRoute` by mapping between `SiteRoute` and URL path segments (`List String`), and prove `LawfulUrlCodec`:\n\n```lean title=\"Proving URL round-trip Invertibility\"\ndef routeCodec : UrlCodec SiteRoute where\n  toSegments\n    | .home => []\n    | .whyLean => [\"why-lean\"]\n    | .ecosystem => [\"ecosystem\"]\n    | .showcase => [\"showcase\"]\n    | .docsIndex => [\"docs\"]\n    | .docsPage s => [\"docs\", s.slug]\n  ofSegments\n    | [] => some .home\n    | [\"why-lean\"] => some .whyLean\n    | [\"ecosystem\"] => some .ecosystem\n    | [\"showcase\"] => some .showcase\n    | [\"docs\"] => some .docsIndex\n    | [\"docs\", raw] => (DocSlug.parse? raw).map .docsPage\n    | _ => none\n\ninstance : LawfulUrlCodec routeCodec where\n  ofSegments_toSegments := by\n    intro r\n    cases r with\n    | docsPage s => cases s <;> rfl\n    | _ => rfl\n```\n\nThat four-line `LawfulUrlCodec` instance guarantees that every `SiteRoute` formats to a canonical path that `routeCodec.parseUrl` parses right back to the exact same `SiteRoute`.\n\n## 2. Pure navigation stacks and RouterShell\n\n`LakeportRouter.RouterState Route` models root tabs, pushed screens, modal sheets, and mobile drawers in pure Lean, with deterministic back-button priority (`sheet -> drawer -> pushed stack`) and a proved decreasing termination measure (`stepBack_decreases`).\n\nWrap your root view in `<RouterShell url={currentUrl} canBack={canBack}>`:\n\n```lp title=\"Wrapping the root view\"\n<RouterShell url={routeCodec.formatPath s.router.activeRoute} canBack={s.router.canGoBack}>\n  <div class=\"site-shell\">\n    {renderPage s}\n  </div>\n</RouterShell>\n```\n\nOn the web, `RouterShell` synchronizes `history.pushState` and `popstate` events cleanly with your Lean state. On Android and iOS, `canBack` drives the system back gesture interceptor so users never accidentally exit your app while a sheet or drawer is open.\n\n## 3. Static site generation (SSG) and commit-0 hydration\n\nMarketing pages and documentation sites need instant first paint and clean HTML for search engines and `curl` readers. Enable SSG in `lakefile.toml`:\n\n```toml title=\"lakefile.toml\"\n[lakeport.web]\nmode = \"ssg\"\nssgRoutes = [\n  \"/\",\n  \"/why-lean\",\n  \"/ecosystem\",\n  \"/showcase\",\n  \"/docs\",\n  \"/docs/introduction\"\n]\n```\n\nDuring `lakeport build web --release`, Lakeport:\n\n1. Compiles your Lean application to `dist/web/app.wasm`.\n2. Boots `app.wasm` in Node for each route in `ssgRoutes`, sends `.router (.navigate route)`, and serializes the exact resulting view tree to `dist/web/<route>/index.html`.\n3. When a visitor loads the page in a browser, static HTML paints immediately before WebAssembly finishes streaming. Once `app.wasm` initializes at commit `0`, `dom-renderer.mjs` attaches to the existing DOM nodes in place with **zero node creations** and **zero layout shift**.\n\n<Callout kind=\"note\" title=\"This site is pre-rendered with Lakeport SSG\">\nEvery page you are reading right now was pre-rendered at build time by executing `lakeport-www`'s compiled Lean WebAssembly binary across all 16 routes in `lakefile.toml`.\n</Callout>\n\nNext, read [Host effects and offline resilience](/docs/effects-and-offline) to connect HTTP, WebSockets, local storage, and clipboard commands safely.\n"
  | .effectsAndOffline => "---\ntitle: \"Host effects and offline resilience\"\nsummary: \"Issue typed HTTP, WebSocket, KV, and clipboard commands as data values, and reconcile mid-flight disconnects without double-sends.\"\nsection: \"Building interfaces\"\norder: 10\nreadingMinutes: 7\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Host effects and offline resilience\n\nA pure reducer cannot open sockets or read `localStorage` behind your back. Instead, `step` returns a pair `(State × List EffectReq)`. Your reducer describes *what* side effect it wants to perform as an ordinary value, and the Lakeport host shell executes it asynchronously and delivers an `EffectResp` message back into `app.message`.\n\n## Built-in EffectReq commands\n\n`LakeportEffects` ships typed request and response constructors that work identically across Web and native shells:\n\n- **HTTP & Streaming:** `.httpGet`, `.httpPost`, `.httpRequest` (custom verbs & headers), `.httpStream` (SSE / chunked responses), `.httpCancel`\n- **WebSockets:** `.wsConnect`, `.wsSend`, `.wsSendBytes`, `.wsClose`\n- **Key-Value Persistence:** `.kvGet`, `.kvSet`, `.kvDelete` (`localStorage` on web, native preferences on desktop/mobile)\n- **UX & Clipboard:** `.clipboardWrite`, `.haptic` (`light`, `medium`, `heavy`, `success`, `warning`, `error`), `.sleepMs`\n- **Typed Native Plugins:** `.pluginInvoke`, `.pluginSubscribe`, `.pluginUnsubscribe`\n\nFor example, when you click **Copy** on any code snippet in these docs, our reducer emits a single value:\n\n```lean title=\"Copy button reducer branch\"\nlet req := EffectReq.clipboardWrite state.nextReqId snippetText\n({ state with copiedKey := key, nextReqId := state.nextReqId + 1 }, [req.toTerm])\n```\n\nBecause `EffectReq.clipboardWrite` is plain data, unit tests and Lean theorems can assert on the exact list of emitted commands without mocking `navigator.clipboard`.\n\n## Surviving mid-flight disconnects without duplicate charges\n\nNetwork drops expose a subtle distinction that naive retry loops get wrong:\n\n- **Queued (`pending`):** The command is sitting in the client's local outbox and has *never* been written to a socket. Safe to send as soon as a connection opens (provided `permitsEncodedFrame` still passes).\n- **Uncertain (`uncertain`):** The command was written to a socket (`wsSend` or `httpPost`), and the connection dropped *before* the server returned an `ack` or `rejected` frame.\n\nDid the server commit the transaction right before the TCP connection died, or did the packet drop in the subway tunnel? **The client does not know.**\n\nIf an agent writes a retry handler that blindly resets `inFlight := false` and re-sends with a fresh request ID, the server executes the debit twice. Instead, model `.uncertain` explicitly in your state (or use `LakeportEffects.Outbox`):\n\n1. Attach a stable client idempotency ID (`cid`) when the user initiates the command, before any network call.\n2. On socket close while `inFlight`, transition the entry to `.uncertain` rather than `.queued`.\n3. Reconcile against server state (or replay *with the original `cid`*) only after re-negotiating capabilities on reconnect.\n\nYou can test both the verified state machine and the buggy double-debit agent patch live in the Workbench on the [Lakeport homepage](/).\n\nNext, read [Reservoir modules and bundle size](/docs/reservoir-and-bundle-size) to see how Lean strips unused library code and all proof terms out of your final `.wasm` binary.\n"
  | .reservoirAndBundleSize => "---\ntitle: \"Reservoir modules and bundle size\"\nsummary: \"Why importing rich Lean packages does not bloat your WebAssembly bundle: five layers of dead-code elimination and .ilean reachability.\"\nsection: \"Ecosystem and tooling\"\norder: 11\nreadingMinutes: 8\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# Reservoir modules and bundle size\n\nIn the JavaScript ecosystem, adding a dependency is an exercise in anxiety. A harmless-looking markdown or date helper pulls in transitive polyfills, locale tables, and side-effectful module initializers that bundlers dare not tree-shake because importing a JS file can mutate `window`.\n\nLean 4 has **no top-level module side effects** (outside explicit `@[init]` hooks, which pure Lakeport packages avoid) and **complete static call graphs**. That changes how we design and publish packages to [Reservoir](https://reservoir.lean-lang.org), Lean's package registry.\n\nWe can ship rich, well-factored Lakeport packages (`lakeport-mdx`, contract libraries, table engines, currency math, capability state machines) together with hundreds of theorems, knowing that **consumers only pay bytes for the exact computational functions reachable from `App.app`**.\n\n## Five layers of Lean dead-code elimination\n\nWhen you run `lakeport build web --release`, code disappears at five distinct stages before bytes ever reach a user's browser:\n\n1. **`Prop` and proof-term erasure (`0 B`).** Every type in `Prop` (`WellFormed`, `SafeSlug`, equality proofs inside `MemoCell`) is erased inside the Lean compiler before IR generation. A 2,000-line proof adds zero instructions to your function body.\n2. **Proof-only module omission (`[proof-only, 0 B C]`).** Leaf law modules (`ContractLaws.lean`, `MdxLaws.lean`, `WorkbenchLaws.lean`) are verified by Lean during `lakeport check`, and Lakeport skips compiling and linking their `.c` files altogether.\n3. **`meta` phase separation (`0 B`).** Macros, custom DSL elaborators, and compile-time syntax helpers execute while building `.olean` artifacts and never enter runtime codegen.\n4. **Declaration reachability + Clang LTO (`-ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -Oz -flto -Wl,--gc-sections`).** Lean emits standalone C functions per declaration. If you import `lakeport-mdx` and only call `slugify`, the MDX table parser, syntax highlighter, and `StyledView` renderer are garbage-collected by `wasm-ld` at link time.\n5. **Binaryen whole-program optimization (`wasm-opt -Oz`).** Inlines tiny wrappers, folds constants, and compacts WebAssembly locals across the linked module.\n\n## Interactive dead-code & .ilean explorer\n\nToggle Reservoir packages and inspect how proof lines vs. reachable runtime declarations affect the compiled output:\n\n<DeadCodeExplorer />\n\n## Retiring dead contract versions with .ilean reachability\n\nDead-code elimination helps you ship smaller binaries; Lean's `.ilean` reference index helps you delete dead *source code* safely too.\n\nEvery compiled module emits an `.ilean` file recording exact declaration-to-declaration references across your entire workspace. When your backend telemetry confirms zero `Version.v1` clients remain in the wild:\n\n1. Check `.ilean` references for `Wire.V1` to see every remaining call site across client and server packages.\n2. Remove `| v1` from `Version` in `shared/`.\n3. Run `lakeport check` and delete each `.v1` handler arm Lean highlights. Once `lakeport check` passes, zero dead `v1` glue remains anywhere in your repository.\n\nNext, finish the guide with [CLI and MCP reference](/docs/cli-and-mcp) for everyday commands and coding-agent integration.\n"
  | .cliAndMcp => "---\ntitle: \"CLI and MCP workflow reference\"\nsummary: \"Everyday lakeport commands, source-mapped diagnostics, negative contract checks in CI, and running lakeport mcp for coding agents.\"\nsection: \"Ecosystem and tooling\"\norder: 12\nreadingMinutes: 6\nupdated: \"2026-11-15\"\n---\n\n# CLI and MCP workflow reference\n\nEverything in Lakeport runs through a single native command-line binary: `lakeport`. There is no separate bundler config, Babel plugin chain, or CSS-in-JS PostCSS wrapper to maintain.\n\n## Everyday commands\n\n| Command | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `lakeport check [dir]` | Lowers `.lp` TSX, validates global and scoped CSS classes, type-checks all `.lean` modules and `*Laws.lean` theorems, and maps diagnostics back to `.lp`/`.css` line numbers. |\n| `lakeport build web [dir] --release` | Runs `check`, compiles reachable runtime modules to `wasm32` (`-Oz`, LTO, `--gc-sections`, `wasm-opt`), pre-renders all `ssgRoutes` to static HTML, and emits `dist/web/`. |\n| `lakeport dev [dir]` | Starts a local development server with automatic rebuilds on `.lean`, `.lp`, and `.css` edits. |\n| `lakeport build <target> [dir]` | Builds native target outputs (`linux`, `macos`, `windows`, `ios`, `android`). |\n| `lakeport doctor` | Audits installed compilers, linkers, Binaryen, Node, and platform SDKs. |\n| `lakeport mcp` | Starts the Model Context Protocol server over stdio so coding agents can invoke structured checks and inspect diagnostics. |\n\n## Connecting coding agents with lakeport mcp\n\nInstead of letting an AI coding agent scrape raw terminal output with fragile regexes, configure your editor or agent harness to launch `lakeport mcp`:\n\n```toml title=\"MCP server configuration\"\n[mcp_servers.lakeport]\ncommand = \"lakeport\"\nargs = [\"mcp\"]\n```\n\nThrough MCP, the agent receives structured JSON diagnostics with exact `.lp`, `.css`, and `.lean` file paths, 1-indexed line and column numbers, error codes (`css-global`, `tsx-attr`, `lean`), and concrete fix hints (such as reminding the agent to add `key={...}` on mapped list children or naming which stylesheet binding missed a class).\n\n## Running negative contract checks in CI\n\nPositive builds verify that your current code compiles. For shared edge contracts (`shared/`), we also recommend a 30-line negative check script in CI (`tests/check-negative-v3.py`) that copies `shared/` into a temporary directory, appends `| v3` to `Version`, and asserts that `lakeport check` **fails** with an unhandled union case error.\n\nWhy test that a broken program fails to compile? Because it prevents anyone from accidentally adding a `| _ =>` wildcard catch-all to `clientHandler` six months later. If someone sneaks a wildcard arm in, adding `| v3` would unexpectedly *succeed*, failing the negative CI check and catching the regression right in code review.\n\n<Callout kind=\"tip\" title=\"Explore this site's own source repository\">\nThis entire marketing site and documentation manual (`lakeport-www`) is built with Lakeport, `site-contract`, and `lakeport-mdx`. Inspect `shared/`, `packages/lakeport-mdx/`, `content/docs/`, and `tests/check-negative-v3.py` in the repository for a complete working reference.\n</Callout>\n"

